    "language": "en",
    "targetFormat": "statement-markdown",
    "statementMarkdown": "We call a two-variable polynomial $P(x,y)$ secretly one-variable if there exist polynomials $Q(x)$ and $R(x,y)$ such that $\\deg(Q)\\ge2$ and $P(x,y)=Q(R(x,y))$ (e.g. $x^2+1$ and $x^2y^2+1$ are secretly one-variable, but $xy+1$ is not).\n\nProve or disprove the following statement: If $P(x,y)$ is a polynomial such that both $P(x,y)$ and $P(x,y)+1$ can be written as the product of two non-constant polynomials, then $P$ is secretly one-variable.\n\nNote: All polynomials are assumed to have real coefficients.\n",
    "rawSha256": "96f6e4963dc7e38821df7c9b61b99a52b8bfb0aa5d664d3e0b0b6e4f35b39f7d",
    "matchNormalizedSha256": "b06f479945e83c9f7823c8ad7bde11a2c90ad85a6eb82c4a51770026f9cc5642",
    "matchNormalizer": "final-match-v1",
